Fix xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland leaving input capture engaged forever when a
client sends Release without an activation_id.

Symptom: the desktop goes completely unresponsive -- the cursor disappears and
stops moving, no keystroke reaches any window -- and the ONLY thing that
restores it is plugging or unplugging a monitor. The compositor is healthy
throughout: its event loop keeps running and libinput keeps delivering events.
They are simply still being captured.

Root cause, src/portals/InputCapture.cpp, onRelease():

    if (!opts.contains("activation_id")) {
        Debug::log(WARN, "[input-capture] Release missing activation_id");
        return {1, {}};
    }

The early return happens BEFORE sendRelease(), so the compositor is never told
to lift the capture. The client believes it released and stops asking; the
compositor believes input is still captured and keeps routing every pointer and
keyboard event to libei instead of to the desktop. Nothing reconciles the two.

Recovery only via a monitor hotplug is a direct consequence: a topology change
bumps the zone set, the stale barriers are discarded ("Invalid zone set N,
current M, discarding K barriers"), and the activation dies with them. That is
why the fault looks display-related when it is not.

org.kde.kdeconnect.daemon omits activation_id on every Release, so with its
"share input devices" plugin enabled this is reachable in normal use: move the
pointer across the barrier at a screen edge and the session wedges.

Fix: record the activation the compositor hands us in activate(), clear it in
deactivate() and after a successful Release, and fall back to it when the client
omits the key. A malformed Release now still lifts the capture. When no
activation is live there is nothing to release, so that case keeps returning an
error.

This is deliberately fail-safe rather than spec-pedantic. Refusing a malformed
Release is defensible in isolation, but the cost of being strict here is the
user losing their whole session with no keyboard-reachable way out, while the
cost of being lenient is releasing a capture that was going to end anyway.

